    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Homeowners insurance in Texas protects your house and belongings from fire, theft, windstorm and hail, but the details vary far more from policy to policy than most buyers realize. The two things that decide how much a Texas policy actually pays are the policy form (named perils or all risk, replacement cost or actual cash value) and the deductibles, especially the percentage deductible for wind and hail.</p>
<p>This guide walks Houston-area homeowners and buyers through the policy types, coastal and last-resort options, flood gaps, discounts and the lines on your declarations page that deserve a second look.</p>

<h2>What a Texas homeowners policy covers</h2>
<p>Texas law does not require homeowners insurance, but every mortgage lender does. According to the Texas Department of Insurance (TDI), most Texas home policies include six coverages:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dwelling:</strong> the house itself and attached structures.</li>
<li><strong>Other structures:</strong> detached garages, fences and sheds.</li>
<li><strong>Personal property:</strong> furniture, clothing and electronics, often set as a percentage of the dwelling limit.</li>
<li><strong>Additional living expenses:</strong> hotel, rent and extra costs if a covered loss makes the home unlivable. See our guide to <a href="/blog/loss-of-use-coverage/">loss of use coverage</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Personal liability:</strong> injuries or damage you cause to others.</li>
<li><strong>Medical payments:</strong> small medical bills for guests hurt on your property, regardless of fault.</li>
</ul>
<p>Most policies cover fire, lightning, theft, vandalism, windstorm and hail. They generally exclude flood, earthquake, termites and pests, wear and tear, mold (except repairs tied to a covered water loss) and damage from continuous or repeated leaks. Some insurers let you buy back limited coverage for certain exclusions.</p>

<h2>Texas policy forms: HO-A, HO-B, HO-C and HO-3</h2>
<p>Texas once required insurers to use standard state forms. Since 2003, companies have been allowed to file their own TDI-approved forms, so two policies with similar names can read very differently. TDI groups Texas home policies into four broad types:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Texas form</th><th>Similar national form</th><th>Dwelling</th><th>Belongings</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>HO-A</td><td>HO 1</td><td>Named perils (basic)</td><td>Named perils (basic)</td></tr>
<tr><td>HO-A Plus</td><td>HO 2</td><td>Named perils (broad)</td><td>Named perils (broad)</td></tr>
<tr><td>HO-B</td><td>HO 3</td><td>All risk unless excluded</td><td>Named perils</td></tr>
<tr><td>HO-C</td><td>HO 5</td><td>All risk unless excluded</td><td>All risk unless excluded</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>A named-perils policy pays only for causes it lists. An all-risk (open perils) policy pays for any cause not specifically excluded, which shifts the burden to the insurer to point to an exclusion. When an agent quotes an "HO-3" in Texas, it usually means the HO-B style: open perils on the house, named perils on contents.</p>
<p>Water is where the forms differ most. Many policies cover sudden and accidental discharge, such as a burst supply line, but deny damage from leaks that continued for a period the policy defines. Read that wording carefully, because it drives many denied claims. Our post on <a href="/blog/water-damage-claim-denied/">water damage claim denials</a> covers this in depth.</p>

<h3>Replacement cost vs actual cash value</h3>
<p>Replacement cost pays what it costs to repair or replace damaged property with new materials, minus your deductible. Actual cash value (ACV) pays replacement cost minus depreciation, so an older roof or a ten-year-old sofa pays out far less. TDI's own example: on a $10,000 roof with a $2,000 deductible, replacement cost pays $8,000, while ACV, after about $3,000 of depreciation, pays about $5,000.</p>
<p>TDI's advice is direct: to be fully protected, make sure your policy has replacement cost coverage. Most insurers also require you to insure the home for at least 80 percent of its replacement cost, and some require 100 percent, so a low dwelling limit can reduce what you collect.</p>
[[tool:insurance-claim-depreciation-calculator]]

<h2>Roof coverage: age, ACV roofs and cosmetic damage</h2>
<p>Your roof is the most likely part of a Texas house to generate a claim, and insurers have responded by narrowing roof coverage. Watch for three things:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>ACV roof endorsements.</strong> TDI warns that as roofs age, some companies switch roof coverage to actual cash value. Check every renewal. Texas rules treat a change from replacement cost to ACV on a roof as a material change that requires notice to you.</li>
<li><strong>Roof payment schedules.</strong> Some policies pay a declining percentage of the roof's replacement cost based on its age. They lower the premium but can leave you paying most of a new roof yourself.</li>
<li><strong>Cosmetic damage exclusions.</strong> Some endorsements exclude hail dents that do not affect how the roof functions. They are common on metal roofs and are sometimes attached when you receive an impact-resistant roof discount.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are buying a house, ask the seller for the roof's age and permits before you shop for coverage. A roof near the end of its life can mean ACV-only coverage, a higher premium or no offer at all. Our <a href="/blog/hail-damage-roof-insurance-claim/">hail damage roof claim guide</a> explains what happens after a storm.</p>

<h2>Wind, hail and named-storm deductibles</h2>
<p>Many Texas policies have two deductibles: a flat dollar amount for most claims and a separate percentage deductible for wind and hail. The percentage is calculated on your dwelling coverage limit, not on the amount of the claim. The Office of Public Insurance Counsel, the state agency that represents insurance consumers, stresses this point because it surprises so many homeowners.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Dwelling limit</th><th>1% deductible</th><th>2% deductible</th><th>3% deductible</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>$250,000</td><td>$2,500</td><td>$5,000</td><td>$7,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>$350,000</td><td>$3,500</td><td>$7,000</td><td>$10,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>$500,000</td><td>$5,000</td><td>$10,000</td><td>$15,000</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If a hailstorm causes $6,000 of damage to a home insured for $350,000 with a 2 percent wind and hail deductible, the insurer pays nothing. Some policies also carry a named-storm or hurricane deductible, which typically applies when the National Weather Service names a storm or the National Hurricane Center classifies it as a hurricane. A named-storm deductible may apply once per season rather than per storm, and when more than one deductible could apply, typically only the largest one does. Read your policy for the exact trigger.</p>
<p>Choosing a higher deductible lowers your premium. Just make sure you can actually cover that amount from savings after a storm.</p>

<h2>Coastal homes: TWIA</h2>
<p>Along the coast, many private insurers exclude wind and hail. The Texas Windstorm Insurance Association (TWIA) fills that gap with wind and hail coverage in the 14 first-tier coastal counties, including Galveston, Brazoria, Chambers and Jefferson, plus the part of Harris County east of Highway 146. That covers communities such as La Porte, Seabrook and Shoreacres along Galveston Bay.</p>
<p>Key TWIA rules to know:</p>
<ul>
<li>You must have been turned down by at least one authorized insurer.</li>
<li>The structure generally must be certified as built to the applicable windstorm building code (a WPI-8 certificate from TDI, or a TWIA-issued WPI-8-C).</li>
<li>Some properties in coastal high-hazard flood zones must also carry flood insurance.</li>
<li>TWIA will not sell a new policy while a hurricane is in the Gulf of Mexico, and claims must be filed within one year.</li>
</ul>
<p>A TWIA policy covers wind and hail only. You still need a separate homeowners policy for fire, theft and liability, and a flood policy for rising water. If you are buying near the bay, ask the seller for the home's windstorm certificate before your option period ends.</p>

<h2>The Texas FAIR Plan and surplus lines</h2>
<p>If at least two insurance companies have turned you down, you can apply to the Texas FAIR Plan Association, the state's insurer of last resort. It offers basic coverage, typically with fewer extras and higher costs than a standard policy. Surplus lines insurers, which are out-of-state companies that cover risks Texas-licensed insurers will not, are another option, though TDI notes they usually cost more.</p>
<p>Treat either option as a bridge. Once you replace an old roof, update wiring or build a few claim-free years, shop the regular market again.</p>

<h2>Flood insurance is a separate policy</h2>
<p>Standard homeowners policies, TWIA and the FAIR Plan all exclude flood. You need a separate flood policy from the National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) or a private flood insurer. NFIP policies generally take effect 30 days after purchase, unless they are bought in connection with a mortgage closing, and they cap residential coverage at $250,000 for the building and $100,000 for contents.</p>
<p>TDI notes that more than half of the homes flooded by Hurricane Harvey were outside designated flood zones. In the Houston area, a low-risk flood zone on the map does not mean low risk on the ground. Read our guide to <a href="/blog/buying-a-house-in-a-flood-zone-houston/">buying a house in a flood zone in Houston</a> before you decide to skip it.</p>

<h2>Why homeowners insurance costs so much in Texas</h2>
<p>Insurers price each home on its own risk, and Texas homes face several at once: large hail, Gulf hurricanes, tornadoes, flash flooding and the occasional hard freeze. TDI lists the main factors that move your premium:</p>
<ul>
<li>Age, condition and construction of the home, especially the roof.</li>
<li>Replacement cost, which rises with labor and material prices.</li>
<li>Location, including distance to the coast and local fire protection.</li>
<li>Your claims history. Insurers check CLUE reports, which cover seven years of claims. You can request a free copy of yours from LexisNexis once a year.</li>
<li>Your credit-based insurance score, at companies that use one.</li>
</ul>
<p>Three or more non-weather claims in three years can also lead to nonrenewal, so small losses are often better paid out of pocket.</p>

<h2>How to shop and which discounts to ask for</h2>
<p>Start with HelpInsure.com, a comparison site run by TDI and the Office of Public Insurance Counsel. It shows companies selling in your area, sample rates and coverage types. Then get two or three real quotes through an independent agent or directly from carriers, and compare them on the same dwelling limit, deductibles and roof terms.</p>
<p>Discounts are set company by company, but TDI lists common ones:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Impact-resistant roofing.</strong> Roofing tested under UL 2218 is rated Class 1 to 4, and a Class 4 roof receives the highest credit. Your roofer documents the installation, often on TDI Form PC068. Ask your insurer before you buy shingles, and ask whether the discount comes with a cosmetic damage exclusion.</li>
<li><strong>Monitored alarms and sprinklers.</strong> Central-station burglar and fire alarms and fire sprinkler systems often earn a credit. Water leak detection systems help prevent claims, so ask whether your insurer offers a credit for one.</li>
<li><strong>Newer or well-maintained homes.</strong> Updated roofs, plumbing and electrical systems can lower your rate.</li>
<li><strong>Bundling.</strong> Combining home and auto with one carrier is usually worthwhile.</li>
<li><strong>Claim-free history.</strong> Many companies reward several years without a claim.</li>
</ul>
[[product:smart-water-shutoff]]

<h2>What to check on your declarations page</h2>
<p>The declarations page is the one- or two-page summary at the front of your policy. Before you sign or renew, confirm:</p>
<ol>
<li>The policy form and whether the dwelling is covered on a replacement cost basis.</li>
<li>The dwelling limit, and whether it reflects today's rebuilding cost.</li>
<li>Your all-other-perils deductible and your wind and hail deductible, in dollars and percent.</li>
<li>Any named-storm or hurricane deductible.</li>
<li>Roof endorsements: ACV roof, payment schedule or cosmetic damage exclusions.</li>
<li>Personal property limit and whether it pays replacement cost or ACV.</li>
<li>Additional living expense and liability limits.</li>
<li>Your mortgage company listed correctly as mortgagee.</li>
</ol>
<p>Keep a copy of your policy and a video inventory of your home somewhere safe. Policy language varies by insurer, so confirm coverage questions with a licensed agent, and get help from a licensed public adjuster or attorney if a claim is disputed.</p>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>The best homeowners insurance in Texas is the policy that pays replacement cost on your house and roof with deductibles you can actually afford after a hailstorm. Compare quotes on HelpInsure.com, read the declarations page line by line, add flood coverage, and look at TWIA if you live near the coast. Getting ready for storm season? Use our <a href="/tools/hurricane-prep-checklist/">hurricane prep checklist</a> and browse more <a href="/category/homeownership/">homeownership guides</a>.</p>
